      "side": 1,
      "message": "The reason why using panic_printf() is, for example: \n\n1. VBAT unexpected drop and hence IBBR bit is set. \n2. EC soft reset.(Ex: reboot console command.)\n3. Before the reboot, the firmware will call chip_save_reset_flags() to save reset flag and write it in BBRAM. \n4. Because of 1, bbram_valid() prints the warning message.\n5. if I use printf(), the warning message have no chance to be outputted to UART by console task because the system already reset. Use panic_printf can make sure the message be printed before reboot.",
